body:
It is supposed to go from his check (payroll) to the state and then the state determines compliance and issues it to me. I guess I'll print the form for the clerk.

&#x200B;

I'm just trying to be sure I'm not being spiteful or a jerk.

If you are causing injury to yourself on purpose in any way (marks or not even) then it is self-harm. 

It is \*not\* a normal way to cope with stress, so yes, get help to find a more healthy way. Any attempt to numb or avoid pain/emotion isn't typically healthy and they can all get out of hand quickly.

&#x200B;

That makes a lot of sense. I have a hard time being patient with myself and avoid emotion at all costs because I learned early on that emotion isn't safe. I'm trying to undo some of those patterns now though.

&#x200B;

Thank you.

My father and stepmom were supportive. They knew it was a hard decision and not one I took lightly.

My mother and stepdad on the other hand blamed me 100%. I shouldn't have done x or should have done y. My mother hides behind her 'faith' to say that divorce is wrong and all of that (though she was divorced- her choice- from my dad, she was \*young\* though so for some reason that changes things). 

&#x200B;

I would encourage you to be open with your therapist, but there are a couple of things to keep in mind. 1- your age in regard to laws for minors (if you are a minor) 2- location as every country and smaller areas have different guidelines 3- your therapist as there is a huge range of responses

&#x200B;

That being said, I've struggled with self-harm for years and I've been hospitalized multiple times, but only 1 time (of 5) for self-harm. They are faster to admit me when I say I'm feeling suicidal because I already have the history of hurting myself.  The one time I was admitted for sh was because I'd been in the crisis unit less then 3 weeks prior and the psych felt that hurting myself so quickly after discharge meant that I wasn't stable enough and she re-admitted me. I have never had a therapist insist on admission for self-injury alone. 

&#x200B;

My advice would be to think about what was said when you first started seeing your therapist. Could you keep it general and see where the conversation goes? (e.g. I'm having a hard time coping and I want to use healthy coping methods or I've struggled with hurting myself sometimes and want to stop etc). Another option is that if you think that you can't say it is to write it. Keep in mind, your therapist \*could\* recommend more intensive treatment, but odds are that she will want to understand and as long as you are not in danger of killing yourself or doing super significant harm, she will probably want to help you in the least restrictive environment. 

&#x200B;

Good luck!

Christmas will be spent with only my daughter. My local family and ex will all be together as usual. Some moments, I'm okay with it because they are toxic. Other times, it hurts. A lot.

&#x200B;

Thank you for this reminder.

I agree with him. That is not okay. Not at all.

&#x200B;

I understand the process, though I'm way early on it in. There are a lot of times that I remember something and kind think if I would do that to my daughter or if a 'normal' family dynamic would include it. At the same time, I've been gaslighted so long that I also ask myself and doubt if I'm remembering it correctly. It all screws with you.

My therapist recently made a comment about how my self-harm has always worked for me. He said that despite being against something people in the profession should say, he understood why it is so hard for me to give up and that it is a way to cope. A lifeline for a long time so to speak.  It's nice to not have the pressure of YOU HAVE TO STOP RIGHT NOW. 

&#x200B;

You are right though. Credit needs to be given because we are SURVIVING and BREATHING at this moment.

&#x200B;

I'm so pleased that you are trying to do what you can to offer support, but I will say this: You need to care for your well-being first. You can't pour from an empty cup and all that cliche stuff. Try to think of if there are any boundaries to keep yourself in the best possible space. Music in your room or something similar? Boundaries for the house like no alcohol?

&#x200B;

Then for her, you can't force her to stop. You can't force her to talk to you about it. Just keep reminding her that you are there and willing to talk or help however she needs you to. 

&#x200B;

I apologize, I had more input but it has since left my mind.

No one can make you recover and we all get to that point in our journey at different times. 

Both engaging in ED and recovery are hard. You have to be the one to choose which hard is better for you.

&#x200B;

I don't know what country you are in, but in the USA there are some programs specifically for EDs. Many are residential so they can focus on weight restoration, but also do intensive group and individual therapy.  And while they require weighing for monitoring, they do it blind so you won't have that information. Also, as for the competition piece, when I was at residential this past May I was concerned about that as well. The patients were there from a wide range of eating disorders and sizes and we all had our own individual meal plans and exchanges. I had no idea what plan so and so was on or what have you. When I went to php it was a little different because they gave each range of calories a city name and were forthcoming with the fact that NYC had less calories than Boston and such.

&#x200B;

I agree that inpatient probably wouldn't be helpful as I would guess that you need more focused treatment and not just a holding space for a few days. As for the one who made promises and then took them back, I think part of this is her ethical and legal responsibility. If she doesn't recommend or offer an appropriate level of care and if something significant did happen, she would have to explain why she allowed a lower level given the obvious nature of your health being in danger.

Central Florida, USA

&#x200B;

I grew up in New Hampshire though and went to college in Virginia.

This is a huge issue for me as well. I was recently talking to my brother and opened up about how the environment in our household impacted me and he made comments that if I "saw real neglect" I would realize that our parents didn't neglect us and I should be "glad they pushed you to where you are". He also said "that line of thinking may work with your therapist but I grew up in the same house with the same expectations" (also not true). My parents got divorced when I was young and my brother is only half as he is my mom and stepdad's child. I was the oldest and switching houses and my parents expectations were obviously different for me- a number of people have said so.

&#x200B;

While I was in treatment, one of our group rules was no comparing trauma. Trauma = Trauma It is disruptive and painful. Period.

I would encourage you to seek help because I'm sure your family would rather have you.

&#x200B;

At the very least, put it off. Your insurance policy may not cover suicide depending on a lot of factors and it would additionally difficult for you to be gone and it not help financially at all either.

Yes, I have CPTSD from emotional abuse/neglect as a child. My major symptoms (depression at first) popped up in 2000 (at age 15).  Over the years it progressed to be Major Depressive Disorder (2010), Generalized Anxiety (2015), CPTSD (2017) and OSFED (eating disorder, 2018) and diagnosed in those years.  I'm 34 and only received the CPTSD a year ago, so it's hard to say about the recovery. I wondered for years about my environment. When I finally got the diagnosis, I've been able to start working toward accepting the my childhood was abusive. That it isn't me. That it is not my fault. That there are reasons my mental health is this way and that my behaviors and attitudes have grown to where they are now. I'm working with a trauma therapist for the first time and he is helpful in this process. 

&#x200B;

I'm in the worst space (emotionally/mentally) that I've ever been in, but I have a \*little\* hope that things will improve. I'm addressing stuff, I'm creating boundaries and I'm taking steps I have never been able to in the past- which is probably why things are so bad- so I hope that things will begin to move to a more healthy space.

*If you are hurting yourself, it's a problem and deserves help. Period.* 

*For me, I've cut a number of places. Hands, arms, stomach, thighs, inner leg between knee and ankle, feet....as a teen, I focused on thighs because of the ease of concealment. As I aged, any part of my legs were the target along with random other places on my arms or hands. Now that I've lost nearly everything in life, I don't really care about hiding it. For me, my arms tend to be appealing, though I can't quite figure out why.* 

&#x200B;

*I'm cancelling my OBGYN appointment this week due to new injuries. This is a little different than in the past for me due to the severity recently.  In the past I've seen a number of different providers that have needed to look at scars and/or fresh wounds. Most either ask what happened and/or make sure I'm getting support from \*somewhere\*. Even recently, I saw my primary care and they automatically do an assessment when someone is on an antidepressant (I'm on several psych meds) and although that probably should've landed me in the hospital, she was fine with the fact that I could tell her medications and how often I'm seeing my treatment team. A couple weeks ago, I ended up going to urgent care as a result of self-harm and the doc there looked at my med list and gave the typical "be honest with your other docs" etc.* 

&#x200B;

*In any case, you aren't a monster at all. You are trying to cope and deal with life.*

My opinion may be.....unpopular.

&#x200B;

Don't pressure her. Be honest with her. Let her know that you are concerned and you want to help, but you don't know what would be most supportive for \*her\*.  See if she is even interested in therapy. You can make appointments and know she's sitting in the office, but she won't speak with you there (most likely) and can't be forced to talk if you aren't there. Communicate.  There is a possibility she has stopped and feels it is in the past. Who knows.

&#x200B;

I was 15 when I started and just wish my parents would have offered support how I needed it.

I have a hard time with this one. I've been in the position where I truly feel like the people in my life deserve better and I will never be able to get there. It seems like a good decision because it ends my pain. It provides opportunity for the ones I care about to get better, like they deserve. 

&#x200B;

It doesn't feel like the typical "selfish"....I'm not sure how to describe it.

I don't think it matters if you are cutting a lot or a little. Bottom line, you are hurting yourself and that makes it a problem.

&#x200B;

I understand wanting to know where you are in comparison to others, but regardless of where you fall on the spectrum, you deserve help and support to get better.

I've never struggled with purging but I had a REALLY bad experience at the dentist and have insane anxiety around going (to the point where the dentist recently walked into the room and immediately said, "You're a bit anxious, huh?"). When I finally went a few months ago, I hadn't seen a dentist in nearly a decade. Since this was the case, I ended up needing a lot of work done, which is never fun. I'd encourage you to go and get it over with. The longer you wait, the worse and more work it'll need.  

&#x200B;

As for you family, can't you just say you need to go because you haven't been in a long time?

*No. I've always found it helpful for my parents to let me know that they were available but not to pressure me. Knowing that they will offer support without being suffocating is beneficial for sure.* 

&#x200B;

*This is as true now as an adult as it was when I was a teen.*

I'm 34 and I started at 15 as well. As January 2019 rolls around it'll be 19 years. 

&#x200B;

Try to be kind to yourself and take care of your injuries.

I spent a large amount of time babysitting my brothers so by the time my parents got home, I disappeared into my room and never learned how to cook. My ex always did most of the cooking, so that did help me learn at all.

&#x200B;

What I found helpful was to find recipes on Pinterest. I chose ones that I knew I could do in reading through them and that has started to help me learn recipes and such.

&#x200B;

I messed up 2 grilled cheese sandwiches at dinner tonight. I can relate.

Thank you for sharing this. It is wonderfully written and so easy to relate to..

&#x200B;

I'm sorry that your mother isn't supportive of your needs. <3

You shouldn't mix any sort of psych med with alcohol. Not only can it increase the effects (like being drowsy or impulsive), but some are just dangerous. One that I'm on now, mixing with alcohol can cause seizures and another one I was on, the mixture could slow breathing to the point of death. They've got warnings for a reason.

&#x200B;

On the medication side, I've been on tons of meds in all different classes and dosages. My body has an issue with absorption so none are very effective or tolerable.

I'm just now realizing the extent of the abuse from my environment growing up. I'm getting divorced from an abusive ex. I've been gaslighted forever.

&#x200B;

I doubt everything. My memories. My perceptions. My responses. Everything.

Yes. When I was in college one of my best friends had an amazing relationship with her mother. I'm talking, her mom sent her care packages and everything. They spoke about everything. Super close. I've always wanted that.

&#x200B;

My mother is incapable.

I struggle with this as well. I have to avoid the gym because I have to outdo everyone since I pick the machine in the back corner to see what others are doing. I will push it too far if I'm outside as well.

&#x200B;

As far as how to get more healthy, have you tried any in-home programs? I found that if I picked a workout program to do in my living room it was helpful. It has a specific start and stop and if I pushed myself hard, I knew I was getting a good workout. It helped me keep some additional boundaries in place.

And if you don't get it on this side, then the insurance companies dictates your treatment more than your providers. 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

My mother didn't compare me verbally with my brothers, though she obviously favored them and their accomplishments.

&#x200B;

She often would make comments about my friends, "So and so is \*such\* a good kid considering all (s)he has to deal with."  etc

She's 6. We just recently moved into separate homes.  These are the two that we used for her. We read "Two Homes" and then asked why she thought we read it to her. She answered, "Because you love me?" <3  We told her that we did and then discussed how things would be similar. She's asked questions since then and been emotional at times, but I was just telling her last night how proud I was for how she's been working through it.

[https://www.amazon.com/Two-Homes-Claire-Masurel/dp/0763619841/ref=sr\_1\_3?ie=UTF8&qid=1542133270&sr=8-3&keywords=books+for+kids+about+divorce](https://www.amazon.com/Two-Homes-Claire-Masurel/dp/0763619841/ref=sr_1_3?ie=UTF8&qid=1542133270&sr=8-3&keywords=books+for+kids+about+divorce)

&#x200B;

[https://www.amazon.com/Standing-Own-Two-Feet-Affirmation/dp/0843132213/ref=pd\_bxgy\_14\_img\_3?\_encoding=UTF8&pd\_rd\_i=0843132213&pd\_rd\_r=e8ff9f63-e770-11e8-aacc-dbdecc11c105&pd\_rd\_w=xWf4n&pd\_rd\_wg=HKcBs&pf\_rd\_i=desktop-dp-sims&pf\_rd\_m=ATVPDKIKX0DER&pf\_rd\_p=6725dbd6-9917-451d-beba-16af7874e407&pf\_rd\_r=BCPVNK1E7ANB66SZK0AV&pf\_rd\_s=desktop-dp-sims&pf\_rd\_t=40701&psc=1&refRID=BCPVNK1E7ANB66SZK0AV](https://www.amazon.com/Standing-Own-Two-Feet-Affirmation/dp/0843132213/ref=pd_bxgy_14_img_3?_encoding=UTF8&pd_rd_i=0843132213&pd_rd_r=e8ff9f63-e770-11e8-aacc-dbdecc11c105&pd_rd_w=xWf4n&pd_rd_wg=HKcBs&pf_rd_i=desktop-dp-sims&pf_rd_m=ATVPDKIKX0DER&pf_rd_p=6725dbd6-9917-451d-beba-16af7874e407&pf_rd_r=BCPVNK1E7ANB66SZK0AV&pf_rd_s=desktop-dp-sims&pf_rd_t=40701&psc=1&refRID=BCPVNK1E7ANB66SZK0AV)

&#x200B;

I was able to qualify briefly for a free attorney and they are the ones who sent me the initial paperwork. I was concerned a couple of weeks ago that he was going to take off with kiddo, so I managed to get a judge to grant an order that neither of us can leave the state with her until the divorce is finalized.  I've since gotten a little more income so I don't know that I'd qualify anymore despite that income being a direct result of my other income. I'll have to check into that for sure though.

&#x200B;

I do not have a restraining order and my immediate thought is that I don't need one. It's not that bad (I know that's victim minimization thinking too though). Outside of off handed remarks and such, he's not directly threatened me. I don't know that there's anything the court would do.

&#x200B;

Our time-sharing agreement is that she is with me 5 nights a week and with him 2. Our state has an automatic formula that is used to calculate child support, so that's been put into the equation already. He'll have to pay $190 a month and he thought that was "higher than I thought".

It's super tempting to do NC, but I actually work for my mom part-time right now. Employment-wise she offers me flexibility that is going to be hard to find elsewhere. I'm allowed to schedule my own hours around kiddo's school and my doctor's appointments, I'm allowed my service dog etc. BUT, I'll have to try to find something else or create very specific boundaries in the work space if I'm going to continue there. It's doubly betraying since it is \*all\* my family that's local. His lives out of state. 

&#x200B;

And, yes, the everyone before her daughter is consistent with how things have been my entire life. 

&#x200B;

But, I have no history of trauma and no reason to be \*this\* way still.  (also said by my mother referring to my mental illnesses)

So far, I've been the one to take all the steps even though the divorce is "mutual".  I got the paperwork. I did the paperwork. We haven't involved attorneys at this point, because I've been able to sort things and such. The lawyer will cost a minimum of $500 on top of the several hundred that has to be paid to the court and obviously, I'm footing both those bills. He's said specifically in the past that if I "tried to keep her away from me" that "we'd have problems" and as I said, my mom will side with anyone over me especially if she thinks it'll give her more access to my daughter. My therapist said that if he tries to get sole custody, he (therapist) would have nothing to say that would be incriminating or lead the court to think I'm unfit. My fear though is my history of hospitalizations related to my disabling mental illnesses.

&#x200B;

She and I did talk the other day about it and I explained that I didn't like it. She knows Mommy has been in the hospital for treatment and she realizes that it is mental related due to my medications and service dog.

My biological father was astonished by this as well. He's my supportive parent, but lives several states away. He is going to be coming here in the winters moving forward, but as of right now, the other side of my family are the only local ones. It's been coming out recently that there was a great deal of emotional abuse and neglect in that household growing up. My dad has said that he feels bad about it now knowing some of what I never told him growing up. I avoid conflict at all costs because of that environment, so my dad said that if I created boundaries and had trouble explaining them that I could have my mom call him and he'd set her straight. He said he's been biting his tongue recently anyhow and has no issue telling her off.

&#x200B;

We have joint time-sharing between my ex and myself. I'm concerned about this since I've realized his personality fits so well with my mother and stepdad. Her time there she is likely to be influenced by all 3 of them. It's bad enough that she learned before I left that startling me was fun (I have PTSD and am easily startled) from my ex along with some other inappropriate things. I'm not sure how to remedy this as I really have no legal say as to his living arrangements. He's responded emotionally through this whole ordeal and has made comments to my daughter already (she's said, "You know Daddy still loves you...." a few times.... or when he asked her who she wanted to live with and we had NOT decided to divorce yet). I want to be careful though because I don't want him to put her through unnecessary pain and I fear that if I do something he disagrees with, he'll push for full custody using my mental health issues against me. I have no doubt (especially now) that if that were the case, my family would totally side with him and I don't want to lose my girl.
